  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The equation ${y}^{2}+4y-9=0$ can be written in the form $(y+p{)}^{2}=q.$   Find $p+q.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+4y-9 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot y & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot y+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y+2{)}^{2}-4 & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2{)}^{2}-4 & =9 \\\\ (y+2{)}^{2} & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=2,$ $q=13,$ and $p+q=15.$",
